            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Study: Why a Screenshot Alone Failed to Resolve the Appeal</h2>
<p>A utility app team had its Google Ads account suspended after launch, with a notice indicating that the ads might not comply with Google Ads policies. The first appeal included a business license, an App download link, and a short statement that the ads were legitimate. The appeal was rejected.</p>
<p>On closer review, the appeal did not address the actual reason for rejection. The pricing shown on the landing page did not match the in-app pricing, and the payment record showed that the new account was using a card previously associated with an older ad account. The team had also described an inaccessible website and a restricted App feature as the same issue.</p>
<p>The lesson is clear: <strong>A Google Ads appeal is a verifiable account of the facts, not simply a request to be reinstated.</strong> Reviewers may need to confirm that the advertiser is authorized to promote the product, that the service genuinely exists, that payment methods comply with policy, and that the reported issue has been addressed.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Identify the Type of Account Issue</h2>
<p>Before preparing documents, use the notification email, billing status, and account notices to narrow down the issue instead of uploading every available screenshot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment or chargeback issues:</strong> Check card ownership, prepaid balances, billing addresses, chargebacks, and unusual payment activity.</li>
<li><strong>Website information issues:</strong> Verify domain resolution, HTTPS, accessibility, privacy policies, and contact details.</li>
<li><strong>Ad content issues:</strong> Review misleading claims, false statements, trademark use, and unsupported performance promises.</li>
<li><strong>Business verification issues:</strong> Prepare the business registration, company address, website ownership, and proof of the relationship with the promoted product.</li>
<li><strong>Related-account or suspicious-activity issues:</strong> Organize records for old and new accounts, payment instruments, login devices, and team activity. Avoid repeated logins or creating new accounts with substantially identical appeals.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the notice uses broad language, refer to the full original email and policy wording. Do not exaggerate or minimize the alleged violation.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Build a Consistent Evidence Package</h2>
<p>Each document should answer a specific question raised by the notice rather than merely demonstrate that the company appears legitimate.</p>
<h3>1. Business Identity and Authorization</h3>
<p>Prepare the business registration, proof of company address, and screenshots from the website or App developer console. Names on documents, payment records, landing pages, and the advertiser profile should be as consistent as possible. If a brand is advertised through a reseller or distributor, include an authorization letter or partnership agreement.</p>
<h3>2. Website and Landing-Page Evidence</h3>
<p>Captures should include the page URL and date and clearly show the product, prices, plans, refund or subscription terms, contact information, and privacy policy. In this case, the App team added side-by-side captures of the former pricing page and the current version, then explained why the historical price was no longer advertised.</p>
<h3>3. Payment and Transaction Records</h3>
<p>Provide Google Ads billing records, evidence of card or payment-instrument ownership, and the billing address. Redact unrelated sensitive information, but do not hide the dates, amounts, or payment status required for review. Never share full card numbers, passwords, verification codes, or account credentials.</p>
<h3>4. Evidence of Corrective Action</h3>
<p>Explain what was found, what was changed, and how the issue will be prevented in the future. Add correction dates, landing-page versions, or internal review records where relevant. If a problem cannot yet be fully resolved, state the current situation and interim measures honestly rather than claiming it has been fixed.</p>
<h2>A Clear Appeal Structure</h2>
<p>A concise appeal can follow this sequence:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Account and promoted product:</strong> Identify the advertiser, product, and target markets.</li>
<li><strong>Notice date and original issue:</strong> Quote the stated reason accurately.</li>
<li><strong>Investigation performed:</strong> List the pages, billing records, or business documents reviewed.</li>
<li><strong>Corrective actions:</strong> Describe completed changes and their dates.</li>
<li><strong>Preventive measures:</strong> Explain controls such as pre-publication policy reviews, regular pricing checks, and dedicated payment management.</li>
<li><strong>Attachment index:</strong> Label each file and explain its purpose.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep the package focused on relevant evidence and use descriptive filenames, such as “Business Registration,” “Website Ownership Verification,” “Payment Instrument Ownership,” or “Landing Page Before and After Correction.” A large volume of unrelated documents may increase review effort rather than improve the outcome.</p>
<h2>Practices That Can Delay Review</h2>
<p>First, creating multiple new accounts with nearly identical appeals while the complaint remains unresolved may be interpreted as an attempt to bypass enforcement. Second, deleting disputed pages without preserving evidence makes it difficult to verify remediation. Third, mixing information from the website, App Store listing, and landing page can create contradictions. Finally, stating only that the product is legitimate without answering the specific issue in the notice does not provide a useful resolution.</p>
<p>Do not fabricate contracts, authorizations, invoices, or correction screenshots. Authentic documents are more useful than polished evidence that cannot be verified. Before submitting, confirm that every attachment opens correctly, is legible, and contains only the sensitive information that should be redacted.</p>
<h2>Outcome and Three Essential Follow-Up Actions</h2>
<p>The utility App team reorganized the second appeal around the notice, supporting evidence, and corrective actions. It also reconciled the pricing pages, business identity, and payment instruments. The account later returned to an active billing status, after which the team submitted a small number of ads for verification. Its workflow was updated to include pre-launch policy checks and monthly pricing reviews.</p>
<p>Restoration is not the end of risk management. Three follow-up actions are essential:</p>
<ul>
<li>Review every ad for unsupported claims, trademark issues, and required permissions;</li>
<li>Reconcile payment instruments, billing addresses, and business details every month;</li>
<li>Maintain version records for websites and landing pages, including evidence of corrections.</li>
</ul>
<p>Google Ads review outcomes depend on account history, violation type, and the completeness of the evidence. No agency can guarantee approval. The strongest approach is to answer the exact notice, provide authentic documentation, and explain practical safeguards rather than rely on a supposed “guaranteed approval” script.</p>
